{"pk": "1216", "sid": "PKDB01069", "name": "Chik2014", "licence": "open", "access": "public", "date": "2024-08-13", "group_count": 1, "individual_count": 24, "intervention_count": 2, "output_count": 56, "output_calculated_count": 18, "subset_count": 2, "timecourse_count": 2, "scatter_count": 0, "reference": {"pk": 1223, "study": {"sid": "PKDB01069", "name": "Chik2014"}, "pmid": null, "sid": "Chik2014", "name": "Chik2014", "doi": "10.4172/jbb.1000185", "title": "A Bioequivalence comparison of two captopril formulations (25 mg tablets): An open-label, randomized, two-treatment, two-way crossover study in healthy volunteers", "abstract": "This was an open-label, randomized, 2-treatment, 2-way crossover study with 1 week washout period between the 2 study arms. Healthy volunteers received a 25 mg tablet of the test formulation or 25 mg tablet of the reference formulation. Plasma concentrations of captopril were analyzed using a validated LC-MS/MS method. The mean values for Cmax, Tmax, AUC0\u2013t, and AUC0\u2013\u221e with the test formulation of captopril were 235.21 ng/mL, 0.82 hours, 329.25 ng/mL\u2022h, and 337.43 ng/mL\u2022h, respectively; for the reference formulation, the values were 228.28 ng/mL, 0.72 h, 315.87 ng/mL\u2022h, and 323.90 ng/mL\u2022h. For captopril, the 90% CIs for the test formulation/reference formulation ratio for both log Cmax and AUC0\u2013\u221e were within the bioequivalence limit of 80% to 125% (81.08\u2013122.78% and 85.19\u2013 117.68%). Both formulations appeared well tolerated in the population studied.", "journal": "Journal of Bioequivalence & Bioavailability", "date": "2014-05-04", "authors": [{"pk": 7059, "first_name": "Z", "last_name": "Chik"}, {"pk": 7060, "first_name": "NM", "last_name": "Deril"}, {"pk": 7061, "first_name": "EMH", "last_name": "Didi"}, {"pk": 7062, "first_name": "RC", "last_name": "Basu"}, {"pk": 7063, "first_name": "J", "last_name": "Ratnasingam"}, {"pk": 7064, "first_name": "Z", "last_name": "Mohamed"}]}, "re